<section aria-labelledby="<%= guideline_anchor(:specifications) %>">
  <h2 id="<%= guideline_anchor(:specifications) %>">7. Specifications</h2>
  <p>
    Each specification has its own field, so that users can filter and compare products by it.
    This only works when all products use the fields in the same way.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <b>Use the values that the brand published.</b> Do not use your own measurements or
      the measurements of a review. If a review measured something different, you can write that in
      the description.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Units:</b> if the field has one unit, convert the published value into this unit. If the
      field lets you select a unit, select the unit of the published value.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>One input for each operating point:</b> some fields have more than one input, for example
      output power into 8&nbsp;Ω and into 4&nbsp;Ω. A product has all of them at the same time.
      Enter each published value into its own input, and leave the other inputs empty.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>"Measured at":</b> some fields also ask under which condition the value was measured, for
      example a frequency response at &plusmn;3&nbsp;dB or an output power at 1% THD. The same
      product measures differently under each condition, so the value alone does not say much.
      <ul>
        <li>
          Select the condition only if the brand published it. If the brand published no condition,
          leave <b>"Not stated"</b> selected. Do not guess, and do not select the nearest condition:
          that claims a measurement that nobody made.
        </li>
        <li>
          If the brand published the same specification under more than one condition, enter the
          value of the <b>tighter</b> condition &mdash; &plusmn;3&nbsp;dB before &plusmn;6&nbsp;dB,
          1% THD before 10% THD. Each field holds one value.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Weight, dimensions and SPL are for one piece</b> and without the packaging, also for
      loudspeakers that the brand sells in pairs. If the brand publishes the value for a pair only,
      write that in the description instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>"No" is not the same as "unknown".</b> Select "No" only if you know that the product does
      not have the feature. If you do not know, leave the field empty.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Start with the most important specifications</b> of the category, for example output power
      for an amplifier or impedance for headphones. They are used most for search and comparison.
    </li>
    <li>
      Versions and options have no specifications of their own. They always show the
      specifications of their product (see <a href="#<%= guideline_anchor(:product_or_version) %>">3.1</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
